-- Passage rg7-figurative-language-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ce88d1e3-6a8b-5f40-ad46-7c5d0d7a5d8d', 'rg7-figurative-language-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Words from Old Stories$q$, $q$Many everyday English expressions come from ancient Greek myths. You may use these phrases without knowing the stories behind them. Learning the stories can make the expressions easier to understand.

In one myth, a warrior named Achilles was dipped in a magical river as a baby. The water protected his whole body from injury, except for the heel where his mother held him. Today, a person's "Achilles' heel" is a single weakness in someone who is otherwise strong.

Another myth tells of King Midas, who wished that everything he touched would turn to gold. At first he was delighted. Then his food turned to metal, and he realized his wish was a problem. Now we say a person has "the Midas touch" when everything they try seems to bring success or money.

A third story describes Pandora, who was given a box and told never to open it. Curiosity won, and when she lifted the lid, all kinds of troubles flew out into the world. Opening "Pandora's box" now means starting something that causes many unexpected problems.

Finally, the hero Hercules was given twelve labors so difficult that no ordinary person could finish them. A "Herculean" task is one that requires enormous strength or effort.

These expressions are called allusions, because they refer to a well-known story. When a writer uses one, a whole story is packed into just a few words.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
