-- Passage rg7-figurative-language-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a13b98dc-7e03-5ab6-8a07-82bb3be47390', 'rg7-figurative-language-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Debate Day$q$, $q$Our class debate was on Friday, and the topic was whether our school should start fifteen minutes later each morning. My partner, Soo-ah Park, and I were arguing for the later start.

Soo-ah is the kind of person who raises her hand before the teacher finishes asking a question. Some people call her a show-off. I would call her confident, because she is usually right and she never makes anyone feel small.

Our opponents were Elijah Brandt and Farida Haddad. Elijah strolled to the podium as if he owned the room. He spoke quickly and waved his note cards around. His argument was a fireworks show: loud and bright, but over in a flash, leaving nothing behind but smoke.

When it was our turn, Soo-ah spoke slowly and clearly. She shared a survey we had done of eighty students and explained a study about teenagers and sleep. Each point built on the one before it, like bricks in a wall.

During the rebuttal, Elijah said our plan would make the school day "a million years long." Soo-ah calmly pointed out that it would end only fifteen minutes later. After that, Elijah's argument was like the first little pig's house of straw. One good puff, and it fell apart.

Ms. Kowalczyk declared our team the winner. Afterward, Farida shook our hands and laughed. "Next time," she said, "we are building with bricks too."$q$, 'draft')
