   $q$Some people call Soo-ah a "show-off," but the narrator calls her "confident." Why does the narrator choose "confident"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It means Soo-ah is shy and rarely answers questions in class.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-denotation"},{"text":"It describes the same eagerness in a positive way that is fair to her.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It is a harsher word that points out a problem with Soo-ah's behavior.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-connotation"},{"text":"It shows the narrator agrees that Soo-ah tries to make others feel small.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-connotation"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It describes the same eagerness in a positive way that is fair to her.$q$, $q$Both words fit someone who answers quickly, but "show-off" is negative and "confident" is positive; the narrator adds that she "never makes anyone feel small." Calling "confident" the harsher word reverses the two connotations.$q$, 'draft', 'a13b98dc-7e03-5ab6-8a07-82bb3be47390')
