-- Passage rg7-literary-structure-pov-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5296ae23-4d0e-5347-8f1c-763346a4aad9', 'rg7-literary-structure-pov-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Letters About the Flying Machine$q$, $q$August 14, 1911

Dear Cousin Walter,

You will never believe what I saw at the county fair! A real flying machine rose off the racetrack and circled above the grandstand. The pilot waved to us from the sky. I screamed so loudly that Mother covered her ears. I have decided that I will fly one myself someday, no matter what anyone says. Did you see it from your farm? Wasn't it the most wonderful thing in the world?

Your cousin,
Hattie

August 20, 1911

Dear Hattie,

Yes, I saw your flying machine. So did our horses. It roared over the east pasture at about four o'clock, and the whole team broke through the fence and ran halfway to the river. Father and I spent the rest of the day chasing them, and two fence posts are still lying in the mud. I will admit it looked grand up there, like a giant dragonfly with its wings spread. But somebody has to think about the people on the ground. If you do fly one someday, please steer clear of our pasture.

Your cousin,
Walter

August 27, 1911

Dear Walter,

I am sorry about your horses and your fence. I had not thought about the people below at all. When I am a pilot, I will learn the map of every farm so I can fly around them. Perhaps you could teach me where the pastures are?

Your cousin, who still wants to fly,
Hattie$q$, 'draft')
