-- Passage rg7-main-idea-summary-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8de43da8-d691-511e-b6e3-fcd4fe8b1c04', 'rg7-main-idea-summary-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Lighting the Streets by Hand$q$, $q$Long before electric streetlights, many cities depended on workers called lamplighters to keep their streets bright after dark. Their job was simple to describe but demanding to do, and for many years it shaped the rhythm of city life.

In the early 1800s, many large cities began installing gas lamps along their main streets. Each lamp sat on top of a tall iron post and burned gas that flowed through pipes under the road. The lamps did not turn on by themselves. Every evening near sunset, a lamplighter walked a set route, carrying a long pole with a small flame or a hook at the end. The worker reached up, opened the valve and lit the lamp, then moved quickly to the next post.

The work did not end at night. In the morning, lamplighters returned to turn each lamp off. They also cleaned the glass panes, which became coated with soot, and reported broken lamps so they could be repaired. A single worker might be responsible for dozens of lamps, in rain, snow and summer heat.

Lit streets changed how people lived. Shops could stay open later, and families felt more comfortable walking home after dark. Many residents came to know their local lamplighter by sight, since the same worker passed the same houses at the same time every day.

By the early 1900s, electric lights began to replace gas lamps in many places. Electric lamps could be switched on from a central station, so cities no longer needed workers to visit each post. The job slowly disappeared. Today, a few towns keep gas lamps on historic streets as a reminder of the workers who once lit the way.$q$, 'draft')
