-- Passage rg7-main-idea-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ab3b0170-94ff-5ade-beab-b854dcc8f156', 'rg7-main-idea-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Nature's Engineers$q$, $q$Few animals change their surroundings as much as the beaver. By building dams across streams, beavers create ponds and wetlands that affect hundreds of other living things.

A beaver builds a dam to protect itself. Beavers are slow on land but strong swimmers, so they feel safest in deep water. They cut down small trees with their sharp front teeth, drag branches into a stream and pack them with mud and stones. As the dam grows, water backs up behind it and forms a pond. In the middle, the beavers build a lodge with underwater entrances that predators cannot easily reach.

The new pond quickly becomes a home for other animals. Frogs and salamanders lay eggs in the calm water. Ducks and herons hunt for food along the edges. Fish find shelter among the sunken branches, and insects such as dragonflies multiply in the shallows.

Beaver ponds also help the land around them. Because the dam slows the stream, water soaks into the ground instead of rushing away. This keeps nearby soil moist during dry summers and can reduce flooding downstream after heavy rain. Mud settles to the bottom of the pond, so the water that flows past the dam is often cleaner.

Beavers do cause problems at times. Their dams can flood roads or farm fields, and they may cut down trees that people want to keep. Some communities now install pipes through dams to control water levels instead of removing the beavers. Scientists who study wetlands often describe beavers as nature's engineers, because their work shapes an entire ecosystem.$q$, 'draft')
