-- Passage rg7-main-idea-summary-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('31c18e49-e861-5c97-8e95-598491c74bb6', 'rg7-main-idea-summary-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Carving a Picture in Reverse$q$, $q$Printmaking lets an artist make many copies of the same picture. One of the most popular methods for beginners is the linoleum block print, which uses a soft, rubbery sheet that can be carved by hand.

The process starts with a drawing. The artist sketches a design on paper and then transfers it to the block. Because the print will come out as a mirror image, any letters or numbers must be drawn backward. Forgetting this step is one of the most common beginner mistakes.

Next comes carving. Using small tools called gouges, the artist cuts away parts of the block. The carved areas sit lower than the surface, so they will not touch the paper. The raised areas that remain will hold ink and become the dark parts of the picture. This means the artist must think in reverse, carving away whatever should stay white.

When the carving is finished, the artist rolls a thin, even layer of ink over the block with a rubber roller called a brayer. A sheet of paper is laid on top and pressed firmly, either with a printing press or by rubbing the back with a wooden spoon. When the paper is peeled away, the image appears.

One carved block can produce dozens of prints. Artists often experiment by printing on colored paper or using several blocks, one for each color. Each print may look slightly different depending on how much ink was used and how hard the paper was pressed. For many students, this mix of careful planning and happy surprises is what makes printmaking so rewarding.$q$, 'draft')
