-- Passage rg7-story-details-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('39b4176a-f7ff-5b5b-bcfd-a15dfa890c39', 'rg7-story-details-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Last Hill$q$, $q$Elias had studied the course map so many times that he could draw it from memory: two loops around the soccer fields, a stretch along the creek, and then the long hill everyone called the Wall. His coach had circled the bottom of the Wall in red marker. Underneath, she had written: Save something for here.

For the first two miles, Elias stayed with the lead pack. His teammate Jun was just behind him, breathing in short, ragged gasps. Jun had joined the team in October, after a summer of physical therapy on his ankle, and this was his first full race.

At the creek, Elias felt good. He felt better than good. He could hear the leaders pulling ahead, and part of him wanted to chase them.

Instead, he glanced back. Jun's face was pale, and his stride had gone short and choppy, the way it did at practice when his ankle started to ache.

Elias slowed until Jun caught up. "Red marker," he said, pointing at the hill ahead. Jun almost laughed. They climbed the Wall together, matching steps, while two runners from another school passed them on the left.

Elias finished eleventh, four places worse than his best. Jun finished twelfth, one second behind him. At the bus, Coach Brandt handed Elias his water bottle, smiled, and said nothing about his time. She just wrote something on her clipboard and underlined it twice.$q$, 'draft')
