-- Passage rg7-story-details-inference-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('454d9019-ef2a-56b0-a01d-bb9c700e5ca9', 'rg7-story-details-inference-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Veering Left$q$, $q$Three days before the regional competition, the Hawthorne Middle School robot could not drive in a straight line. Every time Danielle sent it across the practice mat, it drifted left and bumped the wall near the blue goal.

"It's the code," she announced. She was team captain, and she had written most of the program herself. She spent the whole meeting rewriting the steering function while the others watched over her shoulder.

Mateo did not watch the screen. He lay on his stomach beside the mat with his cheek almost touching the floor, watching the robot's wheels as it rolled past. He had been on the team for only six weeks and rarely spoke during meetings.

On Thursday, Danielle tried a fourth version of the code. The robot drifted left again.

Mateo cleared his throat. "Can I try something that isn't code?" he asked. Danielle sighed but handed him the robot.

He turned it over and spun the left front wheel with one finger. It wobbled on its axle. He tightened a screw with the small hex key he kept in his pocket, spun the wheel again, and set the robot back on the mat.

It crossed in a perfectly straight line.

Nobody said anything for a second. Then Danielle opened her laptop, deleted all four new versions of the steering function, and went back to her original program. Before the meeting ended, she wrote a new item at the top of the team checklist: Check every wheel before every run. Next to it, she wrote Mateo's initials.$q$, 'draft')
