-- Passage rg7-story-details-inference-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4acf18cc-fd57-5b94-9e77-fac47af838c2', 'rg7-story-details-inference-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Blank Panel$q$, $q$The mural on the side of the hardware store on Birch Avenue had taken the whole summer. Twelve neighbors had painted it, guided by an art teacher named Ms. Ferreira, who drew the outlines in chalk each morning before it got hot. By August, the wall showed the neighborhood the way the painters saw it: the corner bakery, the basketball court, the old oak by the bus stop, and a river of faces from the block.

Only one panel was still blank, a narrow rectangle at the bottom right corner.

Ana Lucia, who was thirteen and the youngest painter, asked about it every day. "We're saving it," Ms. Ferreira always said, and went back to mixing colors.

Mr. Sorensen lived across the street. In June, he had complained at a community meeting that the mural would be "a mess." Since then, he had watched the work from his front steps every afternoon, a newspaper open on his lap. Ana Lucia had noticed that he rarely turned the pages.

On the last Saturday in August, Ms. Ferreira crossed the street with a small brush and a can of dark green paint. She spoke with Mr. Sorensen for a few minutes. Then she came back alone.

The next morning, the blank panel was filled. Someone had painted a tiny row of tomato plants in neat wooden boxes, with a watering can tipped beside them. Ana Lucia had walked past Mr. Sorensen's front yard every day that summer. She knew exactly whose garden it was.

Ms. Ferreira looked at the panel for a long time. Then she signed her name at the bottom of the mural and left plenty of room for one more.$q$, 'draft')
