-- Passage rg7-text-structure-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e639296b-1c05-5ffd-b59f-2ea17031882d', 'rg7-text-structure-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$From Old Paper to New Notebook$q$, $q$Every week, the students at Ridgeview Middle School fill blue bins with used worksheets, cardboard, and old newspapers. Few of them know what happens after the truck drives away. The trip from bin to new product takes several steps, and each one prepares the paper for the next.

First, the paper arrives at a sorting center. Workers and machines separate it by type, because cardboard, office paper, and newspaper are made of fibers of different lengths. Staples, plastic, and tape are pulled out at this stage.

Next, the sorted paper goes into a huge tank called a pulper. There it is mixed with water and stirred until it breaks down into a thick, gray mush. This mush is called pulp.

After that, the pulp must be cleaned. It is pushed through screens that catch small bits of glue and plastic. Then air bubbles are blown through it. Ink sticks to the bubbles and floats to the top, where it is skimmed away.

Finally, the clean pulp is sprayed onto a moving screen. Water drains out, and heated rollers press and dry the fibers into a long sheet. The sheet is wound onto giant rolls that can be cut into notebook paper, boxes, or paper towels.

Paper cannot be recycled forever, though. Each trip through the pulper makes the fibers shorter and weaker. After about five to seven trips, they are too short to hold together. Even so, every pass through the process saves trees, water, and energy.$q$, 'draft')
