-- Passage rg7-text-structure-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a3d92be0-ae39-517d-9c45-827d5f737deb', 'rg7-text-structure-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why the Lake Turned Green$q$, $q$Each August, the water in Lake Morrow used to be clear enough to see the sandy bottom from the dock. Then, three summers ago, swimmers arrived to find the lake covered in a thick green scum. The town closed the beach for six weeks. Scientists from the county soon set out to learn why.

The green layer was made of algae, tiny plantlike living things that grow in water. Algae are always present in lakes, but usually in small numbers. They multiply quickly when they get extra nutrients, especially a substance called phosphorus.

The scientists traced the phosphorus to the land around the lake. Over the past ten years, many lawns near the shore had been treated with fertilizer containing phosphorus. Heavy spring rains washed that fertilizer off the grass and into small streams that flow into the lake. A long, hot summer then warmed the water, which helped the algae grow even faster.

The bloom caused problems of its own. When the huge mass of algae died, bacteria broke it down and used up much of the oxygen in the water. As a result, hundreds of fish in the shallow coves died. The smell drove away visitors, and local shops lost business during their busiest month.

Since then, the town has asked homeowners near the shore to use phosphorus-free fertilizer. It has also planted strips of native grasses along the water's edge. These plants soak up rainwater before it reaches the lake. Last summer, the beach stayed open all season.$q$, 'draft')
