-- Passage rg7-text-structure-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cd671948-2a56-5113-a086-093265786cdc', 'rg7-text-structure-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Ferry That Built a Town$q$, $q$In 1852, a farmer named Tomas Varga built a flat wooden raft and began carrying neighbors across the Silt River for a few cents each. Before then, the nearest crossing was a full day's walk upstream. Varga's ferry quickly became the busiest spot in the valley.

By 1860, a small cluster of buildings had grown up on both banks. Travelers waiting for the ferry needed meals and a place to sleep, so an inn opened on the east shore. A blacksmith and a general store soon followed. People began calling the area Varga's Crossing.

In 1879, the town faced a turning point. A railroad company planned a line through the valley, and it needed a bridge. Town leaders offered land for a station if the company built the bridge at the old ferry site. The company agreed, and the bridge opened in 1883.

After the bridge opened, the ferry business ended almost overnight. However, the trains brought far more people than the raft ever had. Within twenty years, the town's population grew from about four hundred to nearly three thousand. Mills and warehouses lined the riverbank.

Today, the railroad bridge carries only a walking trail, and a small museum stands where the ferry once docked. Inside, visitors can see a model of Varga's raft beside a photograph of the busy station. Together, they tell the story of how one small idea shaped a whole town.$q$, 'draft')
