insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('42866663-d7c0-5031-9164-b695773f3a5d', 'rg7-text-structure-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Our Town Needs a Tool Library$q$, $q$Most households own a drill that is used for only a few minutes each year. Ladders, rakes, and sewing machines spend most of their lives in closets. Our town of Westbrook should open a tool library, a place where residents can borrow tools the way they borrow books.

The first reason is cost. A good set of tools can cost hundreds of dollars, which many families cannot afford. With a tool library, a family that needs a pipe wrench once could borrow one for free instead of buying it.

A tool library would also reduce waste. When fewer people buy tools they rarely use, fewer tools end up in landfills. Shared tools are also more likely to be kept in good repair, since volunteers can check and fix them between loans.

Finally, a tool library would bring neighbors together. Many tool libraries offer short classes on simple repairs. A teenager could learn to fix a bike tire, and a retired carpenter could share skills with people who are just starting out.

Some residents worry that borrowed tools will be lost or broken. This is a fair concern, but other towns have found ways to handle it. Borrowers sign a simple agreement, and a small late fee encourages them to return items on time. Most tool libraries report that nearly every tool comes back.

The old storage room at the community center sits empty. With a few shelves and a team of volunteers, it could become a tool library by next spring. Residents who agree should attend the town meeting on March 3 and say so.$q$, 'draft')
