-- Passage rg7-text-structure-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('17a4edcf-0665-5e6e-be24-5b8eaad5c6d0', 'rg7-text-structure-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How Bats Hunt in the Dark$q$, $q$On a summer evening, you might see small dark shapes darting above a pond. These are bats, and they are catching insects in near darkness. How can an animal hunt so well when it can barely see its prey?

The answer is a skill called echolocation. As a bat flies, it sends out rapid, high-pitched calls, usually too high for human ears to hear. When a call strikes an object, part of the sound bounces back as an echo. The bat's large, sensitive ears pick up these echoes.

From the echoes, the bat builds a kind of sound picture. An echo that returns quickly means an object is close, while a slower echo means it is farther away. Changes in the echo tell the bat whether an insect is moving and how big it is.

As a bat closes in on a moth, its calls speed up. At first it may call about ten times per second. In the final moments, it may call more than one hundred times per second, updating its sound picture almost constantly.

Some insects have ways to fight back. Certain moths can hear bat calls and dive toward the ground when one comes near. Others make clicking sounds of their own that seem to confuse the bat.

Echolocation works so well that people have borrowed the idea. Ships use sonar, which sends pulses of sound through water, to measure the depth of the sea floor and to find schools of fish.$q$, 'draft')
