-- Passage rg7-theme-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c0a98cc7-05e4-5ea8-944a-280e23b2f42e', 'rg7-theme-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-theme-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Anchor Leg$q$, $q$Jalen had practiced the handoff a hundred times, but at the district relay, the baton slipped from his fingers. It bounced once on the track and rolled toward the infield. By the time he grabbed it, three runners had passed him. Riverside had been in first place. They finished fourth.

He did not wait for the medal ceremony. He sat on the bleachers behind the scoreboard with his hood up and his spikes still on.

Coach Ferreira found him there twenty minutes later. She did not mention the drop. Instead she asked, "What was your time on the last hundred meters?"

Jalen shrugged. He had not looked.

"Fastest anchor in the whole meet," she said. "Nobody caught you after you picked it up, and you nearly caught the third-place runner." She handed him the timing sheet. "A dropped baton is one second. What you did after it was the other eleven."

Jalen studied the numbers. He had been so focused on the mistake that he had not noticed the rest of his own race.

On Monday, he arrived at practice early. He asked Daniela, who ran the third leg, to practice handoffs with him until the sun went down behind the fence. They worked on his grip and her timing. Neither of them complained when they had to start over.

Three weeks later, at the regional meet, the baton slapped cleanly into his palm. Jalen did not think about the last race. He only thought about the next hundred meters.$q$, 'draft')
