-- Passage rg7-theme-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b9773cda-55f8-5d1a-b2c5-5eea801b38f8', 'rg7-theme-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-theme-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Wall on Fenwick Street$q$, $q$Ana had the whole mural planned before the first meeting. She was the best artist in the youth arts program, and the city had chosen her sketch for the long brick wall outside the Fenwick Street laundromat. It showed a giant sunflower against a bright blue sky. It was simple and bold, and she was proud of it.

At the community meeting, people kept making suggestions. Mrs. Haddad, who had run the laundromat for twenty years, wanted the old trolley that used to pass by. A group of skateboarders asked for the ramp at the park. A little girl named Rosa wanted her dog.

Ana nodded politely and wrote nothing down. After the meeting, she complained to her mentor, Mr. Osei. "They want to turn my design into a scrapbook," she said.

Mr. Osei handed her a stack of old photographs Mrs. Haddad had left behind. "Look before you decide," he said.

That evening, Ana spread the photos across her desk. She saw the trolley full of people heading to work. She saw the corner where the bakery used to be. She saw children who had grown up and stayed. The neighborhood had a story, and her sunflower did not tell any of it.

The next week, she brought a new sketch. The sunflower was still there, but now its leaves curled around a trolley, a skate ramp, and a small brown dog. The sky was filled with faces from the old photographs.

On the day the mural was finished, Mrs. Haddad stood in front of it for a long time. Then she brought out folding chairs so people could sit and look.$q$, 'draft')
