   $q$The word "predictable" combines pre- ("before"), dict ("say"), and -able ("able to be"). Which meaning of "predictable" best explains why the coach agreed with Priya's idea?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The Hawks were moving too quickly for the Falcons to follow on the slick field.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"The Hawks were breaking the rules, so the referee would soon call a foul.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The Hawks had loudly said their plans out loud, so everyone could hear them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-literal"},{"text":"The Hawks' moves could be known in advance, so the Falcons could plan around them.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The Hawks' moves could be known in advance, so the Falcons could plan around them.$q$, $q$Predictable means "able to be told before," and the coach says the tired Hawks have become predictable, so a planned pass to the right could work. The third choice reads dict as actual speaking, but nothing in the passage shows the Hawks announcing anything.$q$, 'draft', 'fc1b71d7-429d-5bf8-8214-cd5d24f0039d')
