-- Passage rg7-word-parts-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8b40a0a8-dbf0-598f-8321-e207b978a0c5', 'rg7-word-parts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Messages Across the Wires$q$, $q$Two hundred years ago, news traveled only as fast as a horse or a ship could carry it. A letter from one coast of a large country to the other could take weeks. Then, in the 1840s, a new invention changed everything: the telegraph.

The telegraph sent electrical signals through long wires. An operator tapped a key to make short and long pulses, and each pattern stood for a letter. At the other end, a second operator decoded the pattern and wrote the message down. A note that once took weeks could now arrive in minutes.

Telegraph lines soon stretched between cities. Newspapers used them to transmit stories about storms, sports results, and ship arrivals. Businesses used them to check prices in faraway markets. Families used them for short, important messages, because each word cost money.

Historians who study this period often arrange events in chronological order. Doing so shows how quickly the technology spread. Within about twenty years, wires crossed entire continents, and a cable even ran under an ocean.

Later inventions built on the same idea. The telephone sent the human voice instead of tapping, and radio sent signals without any wires at all. Today, a student can send a photograph across the world in a second. That speed began with a simple key, a wire, and an operator listening carefully for the next click.$q$, 'draft')
