   $q$The Latin root vis means "see," and the suffix -ible means "able to be." Which sentence uses "visible" in the same sense as the passage?$q$, $q$[{"text":"On a clear day, the far hills are visible from the bridge.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Keiko felt visible about the project and could not wait to begin.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-feeling-word"},{"text":"The visible brushes were locked away in a closed box.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-meaning"},{"text":"Mr. Abernathy made the letters visible by spelling each word right.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-correct-with-seen"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$On a clear day, the far hills are visible from the bridge.$q$, $q$Visible means "able to be seen," as with the hills seen from the bridge and the tiny bandage Keiko could see from across the street. Brushes locked in a closed box cannot be seen, so that sentence contradicts the word's meaning.$q$, 'draft', 'edb3f6fa-e5cf-5542-ad86-5cf94ab240ca')
