-- Passage rg8-argument-evidence-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('815f9064-82a4-59f7-849f-883751b4ae79', 'rg8-argument-evidence-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Teach Students to Fix Their Devices$q$, $q$Every school laptop in the Westgate district eventually breaks. A key pops off, a screen cracks, or a battery stops holding a charge. Westgate's middle schools should offer a device repair elective in which students learn to fix these problems themselves.

The district already spends a great deal on repairs. Last year, it shipped 380 laptops to an outside repair company at an average cost of 95 dollars each. Many of those repairs were simple jobs, such as replacing a keyboard or a battery. Students could learn to do these jobs safely with basic tools.

A repair class has worked elsewhere. At Lakeview Middle School, students in a repair club fixed 120 school laptops in one year. The school estimates the club saved more than 9,000 dollars. Broken laptops were also back in classrooms within days instead of weeks.

Repair work teaches skills that matter in every subject. To find a problem, students must test an idea, observe the result and try again. That is the same thinking scientists and mechanics use every day.

The first computers filled entire rooms and weighed thousands of pounds.

Some people worry that the class would be expensive to start. However, a full set of repair tools costs less than fixing four laptops through the outside company. Others worry the schedule is too full. The elective could replace one of the two study halls students already have.

If students can fix laptops, they will all become engineers someday. Westgate should give them that chance.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
