-- Passage rg8-argument-evidence-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e115c2cd-33a0-53ef-9e9f-311ac1f59a06', 'rg8-argument-evidence-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Install Water Refill Stations$q$, $q$Every day, students at Fairmont Middle School buy bottled water in the cafeteria, drink it and throw the bottle away. Fairmont should install water refill stations in its hallways so students can fill reusable bottles for free.

Refill stations would cut plastic waste. Cafeteria records show that the school sold about 9,000 plastic water bottles last year. Most of them ended up in the trash, since the school has only two recycling bins.

Stations also make it easier for students to drink enough water. The current fountains are slow, and several are often broken. When water is easy to get, students are more likely to drink it during the day. In a study at one university, students who sipped water during a long exam reported feeling more focused than students who did not.

Water covers about 71 percent of Earth's surface.

The idea has already worked nearby. Two years ago, Fairmont High School installed three refill stations. Each station has a counter, and together they now show more than 25,000 refills. Since the stations went in, the high school's sports teams have won more games, which proves that the stations improve performance.

Some families worry that refill stations cost too much. A station does cost more than a regular fountain. But students would no longer need to buy bottled water every day.

Refill stations are a small investment that would reduce waste and help students stay healthy. Fairmont should install them this year.$q$, 'draft')
