-- Passage rg8-authors-purpose-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c15ebd96-1aae-53fe-acf0-d34296bc990a', 'rg8-authors-purpose-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Paint the Walls$q$, $q$The hallway outside our cafeteria is painted the same pale beige as every other wall in the building. Hundreds of students walk past it each day without a second glance. I think our school should let students design and paint a mural there.

A mural would give students a real art project with a real audience. In art class, our work usually goes home in a folder and disappears. A mural would stay on display for years, and the students who planned it would learn about scale, teamwork, and public art. It would also make the hallway feel like it belongs to the people who use it.

Our principal, Ms. Adeyemi, has raised two concerns. First, she worries that a mural might look unfinished or unprofessional. Second, she points out that paint costs money the art department does not have. Both concerns deserve answers.

On quality, the art teacher, Mr. Castillo, has offered to guide the project. Students would submit sketches, the class would vote, and the winning design would be drawn to scale on paper before any paint touched the wall. On cost, the parent association has a small grant for school improvement projects, and a local hardware store has offered to donate leftover paint.

If the mural fails, the wall can be painted beige again in an afternoon. If it succeeds, students will walk past something they made every day for years. The risk is small, and the reward is large.$q$, 'draft')
